[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: The CHANGE header claims the four prior defects were resolved by adding build-metadata invariance, resolve postconditions against model state, a padded range text, and unwrapped error assertions, yet the suite still leaves specification postconditions of parse_range, satisfies, compare_precedence, and resolve unexercised. Missing specification scenarios include: caret and tilde expansions whose first non-zero component is minor or patch, plus prefix-with-wildcard forms; wildcard versions combined with each relational and inequality operator, including bare-wildcard comparisons; hyphen expressions with an omitted or wildcard-qualified bound; malformed range texts whose rejection the contract mandates (missing operand after an operator, missing hyphen bound, malformed pre-release or build segments, invalid numeric components, metadata attached to wildcards); pre-release ordering across numeric versus alphanumeric identifier classes and differing segment lengths; equality and inequality comparator outcomes for the NE and GT boundaries; pre-release exclusion when include_prerelease is False and the range does not explicitly target the candidate's core; and resolve scenarios where an assigned dependency's constraint is violated and where search exhausts candidates before raising the conflict outcome. Each needs a transition asserting the corresponding specification postcondition.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: The CHANGE header claims complete range compilation with comparators plus caret, tilde, wildcard, hyphen, and union expansions, but the operator dispatch leaves dead defensive rejections at lines 252-253 and 265 that no range text can reach: every operator symbol recognized by the comparator splitter is already handled by earlier branches before those raises. Restructure the dispatch so these unreachable branches are eliminated, or annotate them with '# pragma: no cover (assumption: operator dispatch is exhaustive over the recognized operator vocabulary)'.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/lib/semver_solver_impl.py: Eliminate unreachable defensive raises in range operator dispatch: split relational comparison handling into a dedicated _relational_comparators helper covering <, <=, >, >= with a table-driven wildcard boundary expansion, so every recognized operator is dispatched by an actual branch and no dead defensive rejection remains. SemVer parsing, range expansion semantics, repository ordering, and backtracking solver behavior are unchanged
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Expanded specification coverage per feedback: range compiler now normalizes prefix+wildcard (^1.x, ~1.2.x, ^1.2.x), wildcard+relational/inequality operators (>=, >, <, <=, ==, != applied to 1.x, plus bare-wildcard >=*, >*, <*, <=*, ==*, !=*), and hyphen ranges with wildcard/omitted bounds; added malformed-range rejection cases (missing operand, missing hyphen bound, malformed prerelease/build, invalid numerics, metadata on wildcards); added caret/tilde zero-component expansion cases (^0.2.3, ^0.0.3, ~0.2.3); added prerelease ordering across numeric/alphanumeric classes and differing segment lengths via compare_precedence; added ==, !=, and > boundary satisfaction cases and prerelease core-mismatch exclusion/include cases; and added dependency-resolution scenarios that exercise an already-assigned dependency violation with backtracking recovery and full candidate exhaustion producing ResolutionConflictError. New fake unit tests and real dispatch tests cover each scenario, with postconditions asserted through the model/reference oracles.
