[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: The CHANGE header reports added malformed-range rejections, wildcard-and-relational combinations, zero-component caret/tilde cases, prerelease identifier-class ordering, and resolution backtracking scenarios, but several contract-mandated aspects remain unasserted. Range parsing still has no rejection assertions for a target version whose build metadata is empty after its separator, whose pre-release segment is empty, or whose version core is absent while metadata is present, nor for wildcard shapes that are malformed because of excess segments beyond the patch position, a non-wildcard segment trailing a wildcard, or a non-numeric prefix preceding a wildcard. Caret expansion over an M.m.x wildcard is only exercised where the major component is non-zero, leaving the minor-non-zero and all-zero core boundary rules unasserted. Pre-release precedence still lacks the case of two differing numeric identifiers at the same position. Dependency resolution still lacks a scenario in which a candidate's newly introduced transitive constraint admits no viable release and must be withdrawn before an alternative candidate satisfies the root constraint, so the postcondition that candidate constraint merging and retraction preserves a satisfiable assignment is unasserted.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Addressed remaining coverage feedback: (1) added parse_range rejection cases for empty build metadata (1.2.3+, >=1.2.3+), empty pre-release segments (1.2.3-, 1.2.3-alpha..1), missing version core with metadata (+build.5), and malformed wildcard shapes (excess segment 1.2.3.x / 1.2.3.x+build, trailing non-wildcard 1.x.3, non-numeric prefix v1.x); (2) caret expansion over M.m.x wildcards now anchors on the wildcard's numeric core and applies first-non-zero boundaries, with new satisfies scenarios for major-non-zero ^1.2.x, minor-non-zero ^0.2.x, and all-zero ^0.0.x cores; (3) added compare_precedence coverage for two differing numeric prerelease identifiers at the same position (1 < 2); (4) added dependency-resolution scenario where a candidate introduces a transitive constraint with no viable release (beta 0.5.0 against beta>=1.0.0), forcing withdrawal and backtracking to an alternative alpha release that satisfies the root constraint. Fake unit tests and real dispatch transitions cover every added scenario.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: test_real_satisfies_caret_wildcard_cores (line 1856) expects 0.0.1 to fail ^0.0.x, but that expectation rests on a wrong caret expansion in _ref_compile_token (lines 173-190): for ^0.0.x the oracle takes the partial anchor Version(0,0,0) and applies the all-zero patch+1 rule to a wildcard patch, collapsing the range to >=0.0.0 <0.0.1, so check.equal fails with 'Expected False, but got True' although the implementation is contract-compliant. low/semver_solver_impl.pyi requires caret prefixes be expanded on first non-zero component boundaries and wildcards normalized into paired comparator inequalities; M.m.x admits any patch at core M.m, so ^0.0.x spans >=0.0.0 <0.1.0 and 0.0.1 must satisfy it. test_fake_satisfies_caret_wildcard_cores (lines 1430-1432) asserts the same wrong outcome for the fake. The # CHANGE attempt anchored caret wildcards on the wildcard's numeric core with first-non-zero boundaries, but applying patch+1 to a wildcard patch anchor is not first-non-zero expansion and violates M.m.x wildcard normalization; the upper bound must follow the wildcard's minor boundary (0.1.0) so both expectations accept 0.0.1.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Fixed caret expansion over M.m.x wildcards: the reference oracle now normalizes the wildcard body to its paired comparator bounds instead of applying the all-zero patch+1 caret rule to a wildcard patch. Thus ^1.2.x spans >=1.2.0 <1.3.0, ^0.2.x spans >=0.2.0 <0.3.0, and ^0.0.x spans >=0.0.0 <0.1.0, so 0.0.1 now satisfies ^0.0.x as the contract requires. Updated the fake unit assertions and real dispatch scenarios to assert 0.0.1 acceptance and 0.1.0 rejection, with matching 1.2.9/1.3.0 boundaries for ^1.2.x.
[BLAME] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: test_real_satisfies_caret_wildcard_cores (line 1849) now expects 1.3.0 to fail ^1.2.x, but the new oracle rule in _ref_compile_token (lines 173-184) overcorrects the earlier ^0.0.x defect: for every caret over a partial M.m.x body it returns the plain wildcard bounds >=M.m.0 <M.(m+1).0, discarding the caret prefix's first-non-zero boundary expansion. low/semver_solver_impl.pyi requires caret prefixes be expanded on first non-zero component boundaries; for ^1.2.x the major component is non-zero, so the upper boundary must be 2.0.0 and 1.3.0 must satisfy the range, but the oracle yields <1.3.0 and check.equal reports 'Expected False, but got True'. The same wrong expectation is asserted for the fake in test_fake_satisfies_caret_wildcard_cores (lines 1421-1423). The # CHANGE fix corrected ^0.0.x by using the wildcard's paired bounds, but that fallback must apply only to an all-zero core where the caret cannot bump a non-zero component; for ^1.2.x and ^0.2.x it must retain caret expansion (>=M.m.0 <(M+1).0.0 when major is non-zero, <0.(m+1).0 when only minor is non-zero), matching the contract's first-non-zero rule and the implementation's behavior.
[CHANGE] benchmark_2/parts/semver_solver/pretense/semver_solver_impl_pretense.py: Refined caret expansion over partial M.m.x wildcards: non-zero cores now retain first-non-zero caret boundaries while only the all-zero core falls back to the wildcard's paired comparator bounds. ^1.2.x expands to >=1.2.0 <2.0.0 (1.3.0 satisfies, 2.0.0 does not), ^0.2.x to >=0.2.0 <0.3.0, and ^0.0.x to >=0.0.0 <0.1.0 (0.0.1 satisfies, 0.1.0 does not). Updated the fake and real caret-wildcard scenario expectations accordingly, including explicit 1.3.0 acceptance and 2.0.0 rejection for ^1.2.x.
